module PresheafTensor

import HigherMathInference

%default total

-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Presheaf tensor product: the mathematical target
-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- For presheaves F and G of modules over a fixed commutative ring R, the
-- pointwise tensor presheaf is
--
--     (F tensor G)(U) = F(U) tensor_R G(U)
--
-- and for V included in U its restriction is induced componentwise:
--
--     rho_(F tensor G)(U,V)
--       = rho_F(U,V) tensor rho_G(U,V).
--
-- On an elementary tensor this says
--
--     s tensor t  |->  restrict(s,V) tensor restrict(t,V).
--
-- The identity and composition laws follow from the corresponding presheaf
-- laws for F and G plus functoriality of tensoring maps.
--
-- If F and G are modules over a sheaf of rings O, the analogous presheaf uses
-- F(U) tensor_{O(U)} G(U).  The tensor product in the category of sheaves is
-- generally obtained by sheafifying that presheaf tensor product.  Keep that
-- later sheafification/gluing step distinct from the presheaf construction.
--
-- A Cech/nerve check is therefore not needed just to define the restriction
-- map below.  Nerves become relevant when we ask about overlap compatibility,
-- descent, gluing, or cohomological checks; that can remain a separate layer.

-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Executable finite forcing model
-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- HigherMathInference.Section is only a tiny integer-valued presheaf fixture.
-- The datatype below represents formal elementary-generator pairs for
-- Section(U) tensor Section(U).  It does not identify sums or impose the
-- bilinearity and balancing relations that construct a genuine tensor product.
-- That narrow model is enough to make the restriction/index laws executable
-- without lying about the algebraic quotient.

public export
data ElementaryTensorSection : Open -> Type where
  TensorSection : Section u -> Section u -> ElementaryTensorSection u

public export
restrictTensor :
  Included v u ->
  ElementaryTensorSection u ->
  ElementaryTensorSection v
restrictTensor Same tensor = tensor
restrictTensor inclusion (TensorSection left right) =
  TensorSection
    (restrict inclusion left)
    (restrict inclusion right)

-- The named pointwise law keeps the componentwise meaning inspectable even
-- though the implementation equations already compute it definitionally.
public export
tensorRestrictionPointwise :
  (inclusion : Included v u) ->
  (left : Section u) ->
  (right : Section u) ->
  restrictTensor inclusion (TensorSection left right)
    = TensorSection (restrict inclusion left) (restrict inclusion right)
tensorRestrictionPointwise Same left right = Refl
tensorRestrictionPointwise PatchWhole
  (WholeSection left) (WholeSection right) = Refl
tensorRestrictionPointwise PointPatch
  (PatchSection left) (PatchSection right) = Refl
tensorRestrictionPointwise PointWhole
  (WholeSection left) (WholeSection right) = Refl

-- Composition of inclusions for the tiny Whole/Patch/Point fixture.
public export
composeIncluded : Included w v -> Included v u -> Included w u
composeIncluded Same vu = vu
composeIncluded PatchWhole Same = PatchWhole
composeIncluded PointPatch Same = PointPatch
composeIncluded PointPatch PatchWhole = PointWhole
composeIncluded PointWhole Same = PointWhole

-- Identity restriction is pointwise definitional computation in this model,
-- even when the tensor section itself is not constructor-exposed.  This is the
-- type-checking/normalization analogue of what the conversation called
-- "reducing to the identity map".  It is not runtime lazy evaluation: the
-- checker sees the inclusion constructor Same and normalizes the pure term.
public export
tensorRestrictionIdentity :
  (tensor : ElementaryTensorSection u) ->
  restrictTensor Same tensor = tensor
tensorRestrictionIdentity tensor = Refl

-- Composition also reduces in the fully concrete fixture.  A generic presheaf
-- interface should carry this as a law; an elaborator may then use the law as a
-- named rewrite/constraint source instead of guessing it.
public export
tensorRestrictionComposition :
  (wv : Included w v) ->
  (vu : Included v u) ->
  (tensor : ElementaryTensorSection u) ->
  restrictTensor wv (restrictTensor vu tensor)
    = restrictTensor (composeIncluded wv vu) tensor
tensorRestrictionComposition Same vu tensor = Refl
tensorRestrictionComposition PatchWhole Same
  (TensorSection (WholeSection left) (WholeSection right)) = Refl
tensorRestrictionComposition PointPatch Same
  (TensorSection (PatchSection left) (PatchSection right)) = Refl
tensorRestrictionComposition PointPatch PatchWhole
  (TensorSection (WholeSection left) (WholeSection right)) = Refl
tensorRestrictionComposition PointWhole Same
  (TensorSection (WholeSection left) (WholeSection right)) = Refl

-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Intended inference / normalization boundary
-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- A useful future Idric elaboration trace for source resembling
--
--     restrict (s tensor t) to V
--
-- should be able to look like
--
--     tensor operation
--       -> require s : F(?U), t : G(?U)
--       -> result lives in (F tensor G)(?U)
--     restriction to V
--       -> require Included V ?U
--       -> result lives in (F tensor G)(V)
--       -> restriction map becomes rho_F tensor rho_G
--
-- If V = U is solved statically, the pointwise restriction can normalize to
-- the original tensor section.  If two restriction steps are statically known,
-- the composition law can normalize them to one restriction.
--
-- Important boundary: dependent types let the domain U/V appear in the type,
-- and definitional equality can erase transparent identity cases.  They do not
-- automatically invent every mathematical law.  Laws that are not definitional
-- should enter through an explicit presheaf/module interface, a checked rewrite,
-- or the typed mathematical knowledge layer already being explored on this
-- branch.
--
-- Also distinguish pointwise equality
--
--     for every x, restrictTensor Same x = x
--
-- from equality of the two functions as first-class values.  The former is
-- immediate here; the latter may require function extensionality or a compiler
-- rewrite rule depending on the core equality theory.
